# Logging in NestJS

When generating or editing NestJS code that needs logging, **always use an instance logger** (`this.logger`) in injectable classes (services, controllers, etc.). Do **not** use the static `Logger` from `@nestjs/common` for log calls in class methods.

## Rule

- **Injectable classes** (`@Injectable()`, `@Controller()`, etc.): Use `this.logger` (instance). Add `private readonly logger = new Logger(ClassName.name);` at the top of the class body if missing, and call `this.logger.log(...)`, `this.logger.error(...)`, `this.logger.warn(...)`, `this.logger.debug(...)`.
- **Static-only utility classes** (no injectable/controller): Either keep static `Logger` for that file or note that an injectable wrapper would be needed for instance logging.
- **Bootstrap** (e.g. `main.ts`): Static `Logger` is acceptable.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD – static Logger in a service
@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  async findOne(id: string) {
    Logger.log(`Finding user ${id}`);
    return this.repo.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD – instance logger
@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  private readonly logger = new Logger(UserService.name);

  async findOne(id: string) {
    this.logger.log(`Finding user ${id}`);
    return this.repo.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }
}
```

- Import `Logger` from `@nestjs/common` when you add the instance property.
- Use the **actual class name** in `new Logger(ClassName.name)` (e.g. `UserService.name`, `AuthController.name`).
- When adding logging to an existing injectable class, check if it already has `this.logger`; if so, use it and do not add a second property.
